The task was already committed (2b59935c) before the interruption. I re-ran every check on that commit and it all passes, so I didn't redo anything.

What it does: yoyo now warns when a config file has a key it never reads. If .yoyo.toml says modle = "gpt-4o", yoyo prints one line to stderr:
```
warning: .yoyo.toml line 1: `modle` is not a key yoyo reads, so it has no effect; did you mean `model`?
```
It only warns, never refuses, and the exit code doesn't change. That way a key from a newer yoyo, or one your own tooling reads, doesn't break the run. The "did you mean" suggestion uses the existing commands::closest_match helper. The code is in the new src/config_unknown_keys.rs, and config.rs gained only a one-line call. config get and config show warn too, because they read the file themselves.

